n because it filters `ast.ClassDef` nodes. This causes the code generation to fall through to the class check. This discrepancy suggests that the code generation process did not reliably handle class-based solutions in the original implementation.\n\n\nThe `exec` call within `trace_python_program` uses a `sandbox_globals` dict that lacks `__build_class__`, potentially causing problems during class definition. During the class definition, the type annotation `nums: list[int]` gets evaluated, which may depend on the missing `__build_class__`. This missing built-in likely explains the failure of the earlier tests, specifically `test_1`.\n\n\nAdding `__build_class__` to `SUPPORTED_BUILTINS` fixed `class Solution:` issues, but other failures must be investigated. The `IndexError` in `test_5` demonstrates the expected error handling behavior; `engine.py` correctly reports this exception. `test_11` uses valid input; therefore, `IndexError` is likely not the cause of its failure.\n\n\nThe current variable alignment process retrieves variable values *before* a line executes via `frame.f_locals` during the `line` event, and the *after* values from the subsequent step. This works well for function internals, capturing the return values through a dedicated \"return\" step.\n\nA potential issue arises with top-level scripts, where the final line might be the last event, preventing the capture of the variable values *after* execution due to the absence of an explicit \"return\" event. This edge case requires careful consideration within the variable alignment logic.\n\n\nIdentified duplicate files in the root directory and within `app_build/` including outdated versions of `engine.js`, `explainer.js`, `app.js`, `styles.css`, and `index.html`. Also found compatibility shims: `engine.py` and `explainer.py`. These files are candidates for removal. The `app_build/index.html` file also needs review.\n\n\nInvestigating unused code within `app_build/` to determine candidates for removal; identified dead UI elements in `index.html`, including the variable inspector table and advanced view drawer. Reviewed obsolete functions such as `buildPracticeChallenge` and `answerBuiltInQuestion` within `explainer.js` as well.\n\nConsidering the duplication of frontend files, planning to consolidate by removing root directory copies of `app.js`, `engine.js`, `explainer.js`, `styles.css`, and `index.html`, and their respective Python shims. Only necessary, up-to-date versions within the `app_build/` directory should remain. Confirming function call dependencies to ensure no broken links after code removal.\n\n\nDuplicate root files including frontend scripts and Python shims will be removed. Only the up-to-date versions residing in `app_build/` will be retained after confirming no broken dependencies. The remaining files in root are essential. The user's approval is needed to run operations.\n\nThe JavaScript interpreter is planned to be enhanced with Python-like data structures and functionality, with automatic input generation based on function signatures. The Python engine will receive type hint support and revised argument synthesis for user-pasted code.\n\n\nExamining the potential failure with top-level scripts, planning a test to replicate this specific case within the execution environment. The objective is to verify that the final variable values are captured correctly even without a return statement.\n\nInvestigating potential improvements to variable alignment, exploring retrieving *after* values from the last executed line when a script ends without an explicit return. The focus remains on accurate data capture for top-level code execution.\n\nConsidering edge cases in variable alignment with top-level scripts, the aim is to ensure the UI correctly displays variables defined in the scripts. This involves capturing final variable values after execution.\n\n\nTesting will be conducted to confirm the correct capture of final variable states in top-level scripts, focusing on cases without explicit return statements. Variable alignment logic will be updated to address this specific edge case. The aim is to ensure all variable values, including those at the end of execution, are accurately displayed in the UI.\n\n\n`app.js` needs modification to call the specific endpoint for Python execution using `fetch(\"/api/trace\", ...)` when `server.py` is active. This enables Python's full features, including `sys.settrace`, for code tracing. A fallback is needed to use `engine.js` if the server is unavailable. This ensures the application functions correctly both online and offline.\n\n\nThe JavaScript code will be enhanced to prioritize a server-side Python endpoint for code execution, falling back to a local JavaScript interpreter if the server is unreachable. This addresses feature parity between local and remote code tracing.\n\nPlanned enhancements for the JavaScript interpreter include adding Python-like data structures such as `list` and `dict` along with corresponding methods such as `.append()` to improve local code execution. Automatic input generation will be implemented to increase usability.\n\nThe Python engine will be upgraded to support type hints, and code generation logic will be checked to handle method calls, class definitions (`class Solution:`) and variable alignment in top-level scripts correctly. This will require updating the input argument generation.\n\nA test case will be created for top-level scripts to capture the last variable values even if there is no explicit `return` statement. Variable alignment logic will be updated within the Python engine to handle these edge cases.\n\n\nInvestigating the structure of the trace data generated by the Python engine (`app_build/engine.py`) is necessary. The goal is to ensure data conforms to the expected schema by the JavaScript code that processes the trace. Focus is on verifying the correctness of the step objects generated by `app_build/engine.py`.\n\n\nAnalyzing the `step` object used in `app_build/explainer.js` and `app_build/app.js`, specifically the `buildTeacherLesson` function, to identify fields that are used. Discovered that the `step` object contains `stepIndex`, `line`, `nextLine`, `codeText`, and `eventType`. Realized `engine.js` sets `step.eventType` to specific string values, while the Python engine initially only sets `\"event\"`. This will cause the UI to not function as the data format is inconsistent.\n\n\nThe Python engine needs to be adapted to align its trace data format with the JavaScript code's expectations. Currently, it only provides a generic \"event\" for `eventType`. The inconsistency will break the UI's functionality because the JavaScript side uses a more specific structure (e.g., \"FUNC_CALL\"). The Python engine will populate `eventType` with detailed values that match the existing structure of `engine.js`. For example, a function call will result in `eventType = \"FUNC_CALL\"`.\n\n\nRevised data capture in the Python engine to differentiate return events, reducing duplicate steps. Added specific event types like \"CLASS_DEF\", \"FUNC_DEF\", and \"LOOP_ITER\" corresponding to AST node types within `engine.py`. Updated data within the Python engine to populate specific `eventType` strings to achieve data format consistency with the JavaScript UI's expectations. This includes `RETURN`, and `FUNC_CALL`, providing a detailed structure for the trace.\n\n\nEnhanced the Python engine to generate more detailed trace information. Added specific event types like \"LOOP_ITER\", \"LOOP_END\", \"CONDITION\", \"AUG_ASSIGN\", \"ASSIGN\", and \"PRINT\" to the `eventType` field based on `ast` node types. Included detailed operation details in `operationDetail` to provide rich execution data. Also planned to review sub-expression frame generation.\n\n\nExamining the Python engine's sub-expression frame generation logic, aiming for complete and consistent data capture during code tracing. The goal is to accurately trace the evaluation of complex expressions. The evaluation of sub-expression frames will be checked, starting with `ast.BoolOp` nodes, to capture frames in the case of short-circuiting.\n\nThe planned post-order traversal of sub-expressions is expected to provide correct evaluation order. At each step, a new frame will be created. The expression will be formatted. The replacement will track expression evaluation at all levels of complexity (e.g., AST node type).\n\n\nEnhanced variable alignment logic to capture variable values after the final line execution in top-level scripts. Implemented a test to verify correct capture of final variable states when no explicit `return` exists. Updated the `engine.py` logic to track final values.\n\nInvestigated short-circuiting behavior within `ast.BoolOp` nodes. Initiated post-order traversal of sub-expressions to create frames at each step during code execution. Formatting of expressions and tracking of evaluation is in progress.\n\n\nDuplicate files in the root directory were identified and marked for removal to streamline the project structure, with only the updated versions within `app_build/` to be preserved after validating all dependencies.  Planned cleaning of unused code within several `app_build/` files was also outlined to improve code efficiency. The Python engine's trace data format will be revised to precisely mirror the UI's expected format, particularly by updating the `eventType` field with more detailed values corresponding to distinct events during code execution. The current focus is on a review and code cleanup.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"\"\"\napp_build/engine.py — Universal Beginner & DSA Python Execution Tracer\nZero AI APIs. Uses Python's native `ast` module and `sys.settrace` in a safe sandbox.\nSupports:\n- Any standalone function (`def binary_search(arr, target):`, `def two_sum(nums, target):`, etc.)\n- Any LeetCode-style `class Solution:` method (`maxSubArray`, `twoSum`, `search`, etc.)\n- Top-level scripts, recursion, loops, dicts, sets, lists, strings, slicing, comprehensions\n- Thonny-style AST Sub-Expression Stepper with Short-Circuit ('and' / 'or') evaluation\n- Stack & Heap Reference Model (Name -> @Address -> Value) with Visual Garbage Collection\n- Global vs. Local Scope Isolation across the call stack\n\"\"\"\n\nimport ast\nimport builtins\nimport copy\nimport io\nimport sys\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\nSUPPORTED_BUILTINS = {\n    \"__build_class__\": builtins.__build_class__,\n    \"__name__\": \"__main__\",\n    \"object\": object,\n    \"abs\": abs,\n    \"all\": all,\n    \"any\": any,\n    \"bool\": bool,\n    \"chr\": chr,\n    \"dict\": dict,\n    \"divmod\": divmod,\n    \"enumerate\": enumerate,\n    \"filter\": filter,\n    \"float\": float,\n    \"hex\": hex,\n    \"int\": int,\n    \"isinstance\": isinstance,\n    \"len\": len,\n    \"list\": list,\n    \"map\": map,\n    \"max\": max,\n    \"min\": min,\n    \"ord\": ord,\n    \"pow\": pow,\n    \"print\": print,\n    \"range\": range,\n    \"reversed\": reversed,\n    \"round\": round,\n    \"set\": set,\n    \"sorted\": sorted,\n    \"str\": str,\n    \"sum\": sum,\n    \"tuple\": tuple,\n    \"type\": type,\n    \"zip\": zip,\n    # Common typing annotations used in DSA / LeetCode snippets\n    \"List\": list,\n    \"Dict\": dict,\n    \"Set\": set,\n    \"Tuple\": tuple,\n    \"Optional\": object,\n    \"Any\": object,\n    \"True\": True,\n    \"False\": False,\n    \"None\": None,\n    \"Exception\": Exception,\n    \"IndexError\": IndexError,\n    \"ValueError\": ValueError,\n    \"TypeError\": TypeError,\n    \"KeyError\": KeyError,\n    \"ZeroDivisionError\": ZeroDivisionError,\n}\n\nUNSUPPORTED_AST_NODES = {\n    ast.Import: \"Import statements (`import ...`) are not supported in the sandbox.\",\n    ast.ImportFrom: \"Import statements (`from ... import ...`) are not supported in the sandbox.\",\n    ast.AsyncFunctionDef: \"Async functions (`async def`) are not supported.\",\n    ast.Await: \"`await` expressions are not supported.\",\n    ast.AsyncFor: \"`async for` loops are not supported.\",\n    ast.AsyncWith: \"`async with` blocks are not supported.\",\n    ast.With: \"`with` context managers (such as file I/O) are not supported.\",\n    ast.Yield: \"Generators (`yield`) are not supported in the beginner tracer.\",\n    ast.YieldFrom: \"`yield from` is not supported.\",\n    ast.Try: \"`try/except` blocks are not supported in basic mode.\",\n    ast.Global: \"`global` statements are not supported.\",\n    ast.Nonlocal: \"`nonlocal` statements are not supported.\",\n}\n\nFORBIDDEN_CALLS = {\n    \"eval\",\n    \"exec\",\n    \"open\",\n    \"compile\",\n    \"__import__\",\n    \"input\",\n    \"globals\",\n    \"locals\",\n    \"getattr\",\n    \"setattr\",\n    \"delattr\",\n}\n\n\ndef validate_python_support(source_code: str) -> Tuple[bool, Optional[str], Optional[int]]:\n    if not source_code or not source_code.strip():\n        return False, \"Code editor is empty. Please enter a Python program.\", 1\n\n    try:\n        tree = ast.parse(source_code)\n    except SyntaxError as exc:\n        return False, f\"SyntaxError: {exc.msg}\", exc.lineno or 1\n\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        for node_type, msg in UNSUPPORTED_AST_NODES.items():\n            if isinstance(node, node_type):\n                return False, f\"Unsupported Construct: {msg}\", getattr(node, \"lineno\", 1)\n\n        if isinstance(node, ast.Call) and isinstance(node.func, ast.Name):\n            if node.func.id in FORBIDDEN_CALLS:\n                return (\n                    False,\n                    f\"Restricted Function: `{node.func.id}()` is disabled for security.\",\n                    getattr(node, \"lineno\", 1),\n                )\n\n    return True, None, None\n\n\ndef format_py_val(val: Any) -> str:\n    if isinstance(val, str):\n        return repr(val)\n    if isinstance(val, (int, float, bool, list, dict, tuple, set)) or val is None:\n        return repr(val)\n    return f\"<{type(val).__name__} instance>\"\n\n\ndef get_py_type(val: Any) -> str:\n    if val is None:\n        return \"NoneType\"\n    return type(val).__name__\n\n\nclass MemoryHeapTracker:\n    \"\"\"\n    Deterministic Stack & Heap Memory Reference Tracker (Name -> @Address -> Value)\n    with Visual Garbage Collection (refCount tracking across active stack frames).\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.scalar_counter = 0x1000\n        self.container_counter = 0x2000\n        self.scalar_pool: Dict[Tuple[str, str], str] = {}\n        self.container_id_map: Dict[int, str] = {}\n        self.heap_registry: Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]] = {}\n\n    def get_or_allocate(self, val: Any, step_idx: int) -> str:\n        py_type = get_py_type(val)\n        val_repr = format_py_val(val)\n\n        if isinstance(val, (list, dict, set, tuple)):\n            obj_id = id(val)\n            if obj_id not in self.container_id_map:\n                addr = f\"@0x{self.container_counter:04X}\"\n                self.container_counter += 0x40\n                self.container_id_map[obj_id] = addr\n            else:\n                addr = self.container_id_map[obj_id]\n\n            self.heap_registry[addr] = {\n                \"address\": addr,\n                \"type\": py_type,\n                \"repr\": val_repr,\n                \"isMutable\": isinstance(val, (list, dict, set)),\n                \"elements\": [format_py_val(x) for x in val] if isinstance(val, (list, tuple)) else None,\n                \"lastTouchedStep\": step_idx,\n            }\n            return addr\n\n        key = (py_type, val_repr)\n        if key not in self.scalar_pool:\n            addr = f\"@0x{self.scalar_counter:04X}\"\n            self.scalar_counter += 0x08\n            self.scalar_pool[key] = addr\n        else:\n            addr = self.scalar_pool[key]\n\n        self.heap_registry[addr] = {\n            \"address\": addr,\n            \"type\": py_type,\n            \"repr\": val_repr,\n            \"isMutable\": False,\n            \"elements\": None,\n            \"lastTouchedStep\": step_idx,\n        }\n        return addr\n\n    def snapshot_memory(\n        self, scope_frames: List[Dict[str, Any]], step_idx: int, changed_addrs: Optional[set] = None\n    ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        changed_addrs = changed_addrs or set()\n        ref_counts: Dict[str, int] = {addr: 0 for addr in self.heap_registry}\n        referenced_by: Dict[str, List[str]] = {addr: [] for addr in self.heap_registry}\n\n        for sc in scope_frames:\n            scope_prefix = \"Global\" if sc[\"scopeType\"] == \"GLOBAL\" else sc[\"functionName\"].split(\"(\")[0]\n            for v in sc[\"variables\"]:\n                addr = v.get(\"address\")\n                if addr and addr in self.heap_registry:\n                    ref_counts[addr] = ref_counts.get(addr, 0) + 1\n                    referenced_by.setdefault(addr, []).append(f\"{scope_prefix}.{v['name']}\")\n\n        active_heap = []\n        gc_heap = []\n        for addr, meta in self.heap_registry.items():\n            rc = ref_counts.get(addr, 0)\n            entry = {\n                **copy.deepcopy(meta),\n                \"refCount\": rc,\n                \"referencedBy\": referenced_by.get(addr, []),\n                \"isHighlighted\": addr in changed_addrs,\n                \"gcStatus\": \"active\" if rc > 0 else \"unreferenced\",\n            }\n            if rc > 0:\n                active_heap.append(entry)\n            else:\n                gc_heap.append(entry)\n\n        return {\n            \"stackScopes\": copy.deepcopy(scope_frames),\n            \"heapObjects\": active_heap,\n            \"gcObjects\": gc_heap[-4:],\n            \"totalUnreferencedCount\": len(gc_heap),\n        }\n\n\ndef _normalize_input_code(custom_input_code: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Splits top-level comma-separated assignments (e.g., `arr = [1, 3, 5], target = 5`)\n    into newline-separated statements while preserving list/dict/tuple literals.\n    \"\"\"\n    raw = custom_input_code.strip()\n    if not raw:\n        return \"\"\n    segments: List[str] = []\n    cur = \"\"\n    depth = 0\n    in_single = False\n    in_double = False\n    for ch in raw:\n        if ch == \"'\" and not in_double:\n            in_single = not in_single\n        elif ch == '\"' and not in_single:\n            in_double = not in_double\n        elif not in_single and not in_double:\n            if ch in \"([{\":\n                depth += 1\n            elif ch in \")]}\":\n                depth -= 1\n            elif ch == \",\" and depth == 0 and \"=\" in cur:\n                segments.append(cur.strip())\n                cur = \"\"\n                continue\n        cur += ch\n    if cur.strip():\n        segments.append(cur.strip())\n    return \"\\n\".join(segments)\n\n\ndef _synthesize_missing_param_lines(\n    params: List[str], fn_name: str, normalized_input: str\n) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Ensures every parameter required by a user's function or Solution method is defined,\n    even if the user left the default `nums = [...]` in the Input Playground.\n    \"\"\"\n    defined_names = set()\n    if normalized_input:\n        try:\n            in_tree = ast.parse(normalized_input)\n            for node in ast.walk(in_tree):\n                if isinstance(node, ast.Name) and isinstance(node.ctx, ast.Store):\n                    defined_names.add(node.id)\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n    lines = [normalized_input] if normalized_input else []\n    is_binary = \"binary\" in fn_name.lower() or \"search\" in fn_name.lower()\n\n    for idx, p in enumerate(params):\n        if p in defined_names:\n            continue\n        if p in (\"arr\", \"nums\", \"numbers\", \"items\", \"prices\", \"height\", \"a\", \"data\"):\n            for existing in (\"nums\", \"arr\", \"numbers\", \"items\", \"prices\"):\n                if existing in defined_names:\n                    lines.append(\n                        f\"{p} = sorted({existing})\" if is_binary else f\"{p} = {existing}\"\n                    )\n                    defined_names.add(p)\n                    break\n            if p not in defined_names:\n                default_list = \"[1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13]\" if is_binary else \"[-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]\"\n                lines.append(f\"{p} = {default_list}\")\n                defined_names.add(p)\n        elif p in (\"target\", \"val\", \"key\", \"x\", \"k\"):\n            arr_ref = \"arr\" if \"arr\" in defined_names else \"nums\" if \"nums\" in defined_names else None\n            if arr_ref and is_binary:\n                lines.append(f\"{p} = {arr_ref}[len({arr_ref}) // 2] if len({arr_ref}) > 0 else 0\")\n            else:\n                lines.append(f\"{p} = 4\")\n            defined_names.add(p)\n        elif p in (\"n\", \"num\", \"count\", \"limit\"):\n            lines.append(f\"{p} = 4\")\n            defined_names.add(p)\n        elif p in (\"s\", \"text\", \"word\", \"string\", \"str1\"):\n            lines.append(f'{p} = \"racecar\"')\n            defined_names.add(p)\n        else:\n            lines.append(f\"{p} = 0\")\n            defined_names.add(p)\n\n    return [ln for ln in lines if ln.strip()]\n\n\ndef _build_auto_invocation_code(source_code: str, normalized_input: str) -> str:\n    tree = ast.parse(source_code)\n    top_level_exec = [\n        n for n in tree.body if not isinstance(n, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.ClassDef, ast.Expr))\n        or (isinstance(n, ast.Expr) and not isinstance(getattr(n, \"value\", None), ast.Constant))\n    ]\n    if top_level_exec:\n        if normalized_input:\n            return normalized_input + \"\\n\" + source_code\n        return source_code\n\n    # 1. Check for class Solution (or any class with methods)\n    for node in tree.body:\n        if isinstance(node, ast.ClassDef):\n            methods = [m for m in node.body if isinstance(m, ast.FunctionDef) and not m.name.startswith(\"_\")]\n            if methods:\n                m = methods[-1]\n                params = [a.arg for a in m.args.args if a.arg != \"self\"]\n                shim_lines = _synthesize_missing_param_lines(params, m.name, normalized_input)\n                call_args = \", \".join(params)\n                return (\n                    f\"{source_code.rstrip()}\\n\\n# --- Runner Invocation ---\\n\"\n                    + \"\\n\".join(shim_lines)\n                    + f\"\\nresult = {node.name}().{m.name}({call_args})\\n\"\n                )\n\n    # 2. Check for standalone function definition (e.g. `def binary_search(arr, target):`)\n    funcs = [n for n in tree.body if isinstance(n, ast.FunctionDef)]\n    if funcs:\n        fn = funcs[-1]\n        params = [a.arg for a in fn.args.args]\n        shim_lines = _synthesize_missing_param_lines(params, fn.name, normalized_input)\n        call_args = \", \".join(params)\n        return (\n            f\"{source_code.rstrip()}\\n\\n# --- Runner Invocation ---\\n\"\n            + \"\\n\".join(shim_lines)\n            + f\"\\nresult = {fn.name}({call_args})\\n\"\n        )\n\n    if normalized_input:\n        return normalized_input + \"\\n\" + source_code\n    return source_code\n\n\ndef _build_subexpression_frames_ast(\n    stmt_node: Optional[ast.AST], code_text: str, frame\n) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Task 2.1 & 2.3: Builds Thonny-style step-by-step sub-expression evaluation frames,\n    including strict short-circuit handling for logical `and` / `or` (`ast.BoolOp`).\n    \"\"\"\n    frames: List[Dict[str, Any]] = [\n        {\"expr\": code_text, \"focus\": \"Original line\", \"shortCircuited\": False}\n    ]\n    if stmt_node is None or not hasattr(ast, \"unparse\"):\n        return frames\n\n    def eval_node(node: ast.AST) -> Any:\n        code_obj = compile(ast.Expression(node), \"<expr>\", \"eval\")\n        return eval(code_obj, frame.f_globals, frame.f_locals)\n\n    target_expr: Optional[ast.AST] = None\n    prefix = \"\"\n    suffix = \"\"\n\n    if isinstance(stmt_node, ast.Assign) and len(stmt_node.targets) == 1:\n        lhs = ast.unparse(stmt_node.targets[0])\n        prefix = f\"{lhs} = \"\n        target_expr = stmt_node.value\n    elif isinstance(stmt_node, ast.AugAssign):\n        lhs = ast.unparse(stmt_node.target)\n        op_map = {ast.Add: \"+=\", ast.Sub: \"-=\", ast.Mult: \"*=\", ast.FloorDiv: \"//=\", ast.Mod: \"%=\"}\n        op_str = op_map.get(type(stmt_node.op), \"+=\")\n        prefix = f\"{lhs} {op_str} \"\n        target_expr = stmt_node.value\n    elif isinstance(stmt_node, (ast.If, ast.While)):\n        kw = \"if\" if isinstance(stmt_node, ast.If) else \"while\"\n        prefix = f\"{kw} \"\n        suffix = \":\"\n        target_expr = stmt_node.test\n    elif isinstance(stmt_node, ast.Return) and stmt_node.value is not None:\n        prefix = \"return \"\n        target_expr = stmt_node.value\n\n    if target_expr is None:\n        return frames\n\n    # Task 2.3: Handle Short-Circuit BoolOp (`and` / `or`) sequentially!\n    if isinstance(target_expr, ast.BoolOp):\n        is_and = isinstance(target_expr.op, ast.And)\n        op_word = \" and \" if is_and else \" or \"\n        clause_strs = [ast.unparse(v) for v in target_expr.values]\n\n        evaluated_parts: List[str] = []\n        for idx, clause_node in enumerate(target_expr.values):\n            try:\n                c_val = eval_node(clause_node)\n                c_repr = format_py_val(c_val)\n                evaluated_parts.append(c_repr)\n                remaining = clause_strs[idx + 1 :]\n                combined = op_word.join(evaluated_parts + remaining)\n                frames.append(\n                    {\n                        \"expr\": f\"{prefix}{combined}{suffix}\",\n                        \"focus\": f\"Evaluated `{clause_strs[idx]}` → `{c_repr}`\",\n                        \"shortCircuited\": False,\n                    }\n                )\n                if is_and and not bool(c_val) and remaining:\n                    frames.append(\n                        {\n                            \"expr\": f\"{prefix}False{suffix}\",\n                            \"focus\": f\"Short-circuited `and`: `{clause_strs[idx]}` is False, skipping `{' and '.join(remaining)}`\",\n                            \"shortCircuited\": True,\n                        }\n                    )\n                    return frames\n                if (not is_and) and bool(c_val) and remaining:\n                    frames.append(\n                        {\n                            \"expr\": f\"{prefix}True{suffix}\",\n                            \"focus\": f\"Short-circuited `or`: `{clause_strs[idx]}` is True, skipping `{' or '.join(remaining)}`\",\n                            \"shortCircuited\": True,\n                        }\n                    )\n                    return frames\n            except Exception:\n                break\n\n        try:\n            final_bool = bool(eval_node(target_expr))\n            frames.append(\n                {\n                    \"expr\": f\"{prefix}{format_py_val(final_bool)}{suffix}\",\n                    \"focus\": f\"Final boolean result: `{final_bool}`\",\n                    \"shortCircuited\": False,\n                }\n            )\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        return frames\n\n    # Post-order AST sub-expression reduction (inner-first, like Thonny IDE!)\n    working = ast.unparse(target_expr)\n    sub_nodes: List[ast.AST] = []\n\n    def collect_postorder(n: ast.AST):\n        for child in ast.iter_child_nodes(n):\n            collect_postorder(child)\n        if isinstance(n, (ast.Name, ast.Subscript, ast.Call, ast.BinOp, ast.UnaryOp, ast.Compare)):\n            sub_nodes.append(n)\n\n    collect_postorder(target_expr)\n\n    for sub in sub_nodes:\n        if isinstance(sub, ast.Name):\n            if sub.id not in frame.f_locals:\n                continue\n            val = frame.f_locals[sub.id]\n            if isinstance(val, (list, dict, set)) or callable(val) or isinstance(val, type):\n                continue\n        sub_src = ast.unparse(sub)\n        if not sub_src or sub_src not in working:\n            continue\n        try:\n            val = eval_node(sub)\n            if callable(val) or isinstance(val, type):\n                continue\n            val_str = format_py_val(val)\n            if val_str != sub_src:\n                new_working = working.replace(sub_src, val_str, 1)\n                new_full = f\"{prefix}{new_working}{suffix}\"\n                if new_full != frames[-1][\"expr\"]:\n                    frames.append(\n                        {\n                            \"expr\": new_full,\n                            \"focus\": f\"Evaluate `{sub_src}` → `{val_str}`\",\n                            \"shortCircuited\": False,\n                        }\n                    )\n                    working = new_working\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n    try:\n        final_val = eval_node(target_expr)\n        final_str = format_py_val(final_val)\n        final_line = f\"{prefix}{final_str}{suffix}\"\n        if frames[-1][\"expr\"] != final_line:\n            frames.append(\n                {\n                    \"expr\": final_line,\n                    \"focus\": f\"Result: `{final_str}`\",\n                    \"shortCircuited\": False,\n                }\n            )\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n\n    return frames\n\n\ndef trace_python_program(\n    source_code: str,\n    custom_input_code: str = \"\",\n    max_steps: int = 400,\n) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Executes supported Python code step-by-step using `sys.settrace` and AST inspection.\n    \"\"\"\n    is_valid, err_msg, err_line = validate_python_support(source_code)\n    raw_lines = source_code.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\")\n\n    if not is_valid:\n        return {\n            \"ok\": False,\n            \"errorType\": \"VALIDATION_ERROR\",\n            \"errorMessage\": err_msg,\n            \"errorLine\": err_line,\n            \"rawLines\": raw_lines,\n            \"steps\": [],\n        }\n\n    normalized_input = _normalize_input_code(custom_input_code)\n    if normalized_input:\n        in_valid, in_err, in_line = validate_python_support(normalized_input)\n        if not in_valid:\n            return {\n                \"ok\": False,\n                \"errorType\": \"INPUT_ERROR\",\n                \"errorMessage\": f\"Invalid Input Playground value: {in_err}\",\n                \"errorLine\": in_line,\n                \"rawLines\": raw_lines,\n                \"steps\": [],\n            }\n\n    full_code = _build_auto_invocation_code(source_code, normalized_input)\n    full_raw_lines = full_code.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\")\n\n    tree = ast.parse(full_code)\n    line_ast_map: Dict[int, ast.AST] = {}\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if hasattr(node, \"lineno\") and isinstance(\n            node,\n            (\n                ast.Assign,\n                ast.AugAssign,\n                ast.AnnAssign,\n                ast.If,\n                ast.For,\n                ast.While,\n                ast.Return,\n                ast.FunctionDef,\n                ast.ClassDef,\n                ast.Expr,\n                ast.Break,\n                ast.Continue,\n            ),\n        ):\n            if node.lineno not in line_ast_map:\n                line_ast_map[node.lineno] = node\n\n    steps: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []\n    stdout_buf = io.StringIO()\n    prev_vars_by_frame: Dict[int, Dict[str, Any]] = {}\n    loop_iter_counts: Dict[Tuple[int, int], int] = {}\n    heap_tracker = MemoryHeapTracker()\n\n    def eval_in_frame(expr_node: ast.AST, frame) -> Any:\n        code_obj = compile(ast.Expression(expr_node), \"<expr>\", \"eval\")\n        return eval(code_obj, frame.f_globals, frame.f_locals)\n\n    def extract_clean_vars(f_locals: Dict[str, Any]) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n        out: Dict[str, Any] = {}\n        for k, v in f_locals.items():\n            if k.startswith(\"__\") or k in (\"self\", \"Solution\"):\n                continue\n            if callable(v) or isinstance(v, type):\n                continue\n            out[k] = copy.deepcopy(v) if isinstance(v, (list, dict, set)) else v\n        return out\n\n    def capture_all_scope_frames(active_frame, step_idx: int) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:\n        chain = []\n        cur = active_frame\n        while cur is not None:\n            if cur.f_code.co_filename == \"<user_code>\":\n                chain.append(cur)\n            cur = cur.f_back\n        chain.reverse()\n\n        scope_frames = []\n        for idx, fr in enumerate(chain):\n            fn_name = fr.f_code.co_name\n            is_global = fn_name == \"<module>\"\n            is_active = idx == len(chain) - 1\n            vars_dict = extract_clean_vars(fr.f_locals)\n            var_rows = []\n            for k, v in vars_dict.items():\n                orig_obj = fr.f_locals.get(k, v)\n                addr = heap_tracker.get_or_allocate(orig_obj, step_idx)\n                var_rows.append(\n                    {\n                        \"name\": k,\n                        \"address\": addr,\n                        \"current\": format_py_val(v),\n                        \"rawValue\": v if isinstance(v, (int, float, bool, str, list)) else format_py_val(v),\n                        \"type\": get_py_type(v),\n                        \"status\": \"unchanged\",\n                    }\n                )\n            scope_frames.append(\n                {\n                    \"scopeId\": \"global\" if is_global else f\"local_{idx}_{fn_name}\",\n                    \"scopeType\": \"GLOBAL\" if is_global else \"LOCAL\",\n                    \"title\": \"Global Scope\" if is_global else f\"Local Scope: {fn_name}()\",\n                    \"functionName\": \"Global Scope\" if is_global else f\"{fn_name}()\",\n                    \"isActive\": is_active,\n                    \"variables\": var_rows,\n                }\n            )\n        return scope_frames\n\n    def record_snapshot(frame, event: str, arg: Any):\n        if len(steps) >= max_steps:\n            raise TimeoutError(\n                f\"Execution exceeded maximum step limit ({max_steps} steps). Possible infinite loop.\"\n            )\n\n        lineno = frame.f_lineno\n        if lineno > len(raw_lines):\n            return\n        code_text = full_raw_lines[lineno - 1].strip() if 1 <= lineno <= len(full_raw_lines) else \"\"\n        if not code_text or code_text.startswith(\"#\"):\n            return\n\n        # If this is a \"return\" event on a `return ...` line that we just recorded on the \"line\" event,\n        # attach the returnValue and post-execution variables rather than duplicating the return step.\n        if event == \"return\" and steps and steps[-1][\"line\"] == lineno and steps[-1][\"eventType\"] == \"RET